{"title":"2024 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagrams\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagrams</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Simplest)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Simplest)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#*\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">*</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Higher_End_Mathematics)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Higher End Mathematics)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy_(Fast_and_Easy_⚡️🚀)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡️🚀)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Innovative_Minds\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Innovative Minds</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Diagrams_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagrams</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A bee is moving in three-dimensional space. A fair six-sided die with faces labeled <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/9/449c60cab43b50481f025124e9d9fe8bc8398b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^+, A^-, B^+, B^-, C^+,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"166\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/1/1d1021c1acac47919e9327c87430c1fe1caf5617.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C^-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" /> is rolled. Suppose the bee occupies the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/e/e3e4ce6135f44658120400b12106ad7ced4cd72f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,b,c).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" /> If the die shows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/c/cecaf8462374d72a6958d4348262397244c8e169.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^+$\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" />, then the bee moves to the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/ccadd18061c7c34f4f794faaca78063b93a2749c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a+1,b,c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" /> and if the die shows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/7/6d7c25a05e358e61a0ca3d51cd9b43908a14428f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^-,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"20\" /> then the bee moves to the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/f/84f3b75075c0a15aedc6d0827cecdf971b40260e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a-1,b,c).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" /> Analogous moves are made with the other four outcomes. Suppose the bee starts at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> and the die is rolled four times. What is the probability that the bee traverses four distinct edges of some unit cube?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/8/0a8e8464b679a95e564f41d949dc5bdde7760e7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\frac{1}{54}\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }\\frac{7}{54}\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{1}{6}\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }\\frac{5}{18}\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }\\frac{2}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"402\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagrams\">Diagrams</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagrams\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Diagrams have been moved to the bottom of the solutions.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Simplest.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Simplest)\">Solution 1 (Simplest)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Simplest)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We start by imagining the three dimensional plane.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Pic1.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/c/c7/Pic1.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"484\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Notice how the three dimensional plane is split into 8 different regions. There exists 8 cubes with one vertex on the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />. We need to consider each of these cases.\n</p><p>We arbitrarily take a cube from one region of the three dimensional plane.\n</p><p>Below is a sample drawing.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Q241.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2e/Q241.png/650px-Q241.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"512\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/2e/Q241.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Roll 1: From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, there are 3 favorable outcomes that can occur. They are listed below.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Q242.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9c/Q242.png/650px-Q242.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"524\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/9/9c/Q242.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Roll 2: WLOG, say the bee moved up one, there are then 2 ways the bee can go.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Q243.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2a/Q243.png/650px-Q243.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"524\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/2a/Q243.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Roll 3: WLOG, say the bee moved forward one. From this point, there is again 2 ways the bee can go.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Q244.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/70/Q244.png/650px-Q244.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"524\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/7/70/Q244.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Roll 4: Finally, WLOG, say the bee moves to the left one. From that point, there are still 2 ways the bee can go.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Q245.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/8/8a/Q245.png/650px-Q245.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"650\" height=\"370\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/8/8a/Q245.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The total probability for one cube is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/5/5a52aefc32f41d14cefdb7239613f653e96e30f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2}{6^4} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"132\" height=\"38\" />. We have 8 of these cubes, which gives us a total probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/3/a13d7b6a28ade0157ffed462f96856e7713c5b7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{8}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>However, we have overcounted. Notice that if the bee moved down one instead of left one at roll 3, it would only have one way. This is because the bee has a choice between either the origin or a unique point. This will always occur on the third move, and because we have 8 cubes(The 3d graph has one for each quadrant), the probability of this happening is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8bec610e35ec986204eb8ffc7b073ac042e5380c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3 \\cdot 8}{6^4} = \\frac{1}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" />*.\n</p><p>Our probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/a/21a0782cdce1d8f3974af8fb2957075e2d26a03c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{8}{54} - \\frac{1}{54} =$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/9/43932adeab5cd9e028041cd6bf2062537827a95f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) }\\frac{7}{54}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation ~Minor edit by lucassf12\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\".2A\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"*\">*</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: *\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>One might question this logic here, onto why it is that we multiply the moves by the number of cubes over the number of rolls.\n</p><p>The theory comes from expected value. It is certain that the bee will be selective in a situation where it only has one choice every 3 moves. This is due to the figure being a cube. Then, the expected probability of this happening is the moves multiplied by the single probability that this case appears, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b434cc056bf004a266516a536c6f8f6c417ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{6^4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"19\" height=\"37\" />. Finally, we multiply by 8, obtaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8bec610e35ec986204eb8ffc7b073ac042e5380c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3 \\cdot 8}{6^4} = \\frac{1}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" /> and continuing as follows.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Higher_End_Mathematics.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Higher_End_Mathematics)\">Solution 2 (Higher End Mathematics)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Higher End Mathematics)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>I put this as an experiment. If anything feels off or you don't understand, do not worry. This is very high end mathematics, and it is fine to feel confused. Solution 1 and the below solutions are much easier than this.\n</p><p>Here, we explore the topological (study of bendable figures in space) definition of this problem.\n</p><p>Consider an \\( n \\)-dimensional torus that the bee travels upon. Then, this torus must be an \\( 8 \\times 8 \\times 8 \\), as there are \\( 8 \\) three-dimensional cubes. The reason we can relate this to a torus is due to the physique of the \\( 3D \\) plane. \n</p><p>Then, we wish to find the ways to get from \\( (0,0,0) \\) to \\( (0,0,0) \\) in \\( 512 \\) moves. That isn't too difficult. In topology, the number of ways to do this is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/c/4fcce1a50f6f8550f1181b96f3e7c71ff5fa4ee2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{\\gcd(x,y,z,8) = 1} \\binom{8}{x} \\binom{8}{y} \\binom{8}{z}\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"56\" />\n</p><p>Summing over all values \\( x, y, \\) and \\( z \\) gives \\( 2^{21} \\times 7 \\).\n</p><p>Now, the total. The total number of moves that exist are all \\( 512 \\) plausible moves upon each probable space such that not all \\( x, y, z \\) are in \\( \\gcd \\) relation of \\( 1 \\) with \\( 8 \\). There is then \\( 3^{512} \\) different ways to permute \\( x, y, \\) and \\( z \\). However, not all of these are plausible, as \\( x + y + z = 512 \\). These coordinates then force their way back to the origin due to the fact \\( x \\equiv y \\equiv z \\equiv 0 \\pmod{8} \\). Then, we can continue with roots of unity to filter out these multiples of \\( 8 \\). Our standard divisibility counter must satisfy \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/2/87266a7c5816f092ef57c408f0501372be600573.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathbf{1}_{x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{8}} = \\frac{1}{8} \\sum_{j=0}^7 \\omega^{jx}, \\quad \\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 8}\\]\" width=\"292\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>(Recall that this bolded 1 is the indicator function).\n</p><p>Application to all coordinates satisfies \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/7/d77398f9ba9bd8bb01d6cb3e1e7d9ab945451d49.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[N = \\frac{1}{8^3} \\sum_{a,b,c=0}^{7} \\sum_{x+y+z=512} \\binom{512}{x, y, z} \\omega^{ax+by+cz}\\]\" width=\"331\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>Through the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multinomial_Theorem\" title=\"Multinomial Theorem\">Multinomial Theorem</a>, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/7/617a1fec58c7e2f5ba71931d9d4847a6230f0866.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{x+y+z=512} \\binom{512}{x, y, z} \\omega^{ax+by+cz} = (\\omega^a + \\omega^b + \\omega^c)^{512}\\]\" width=\"380\" height=\"54\" />\n</p><p>is simplified, and thus we obtain \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/f/defde080ed9198ad773a90395fc35f9841a130ba.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[N = \\frac{1}{512} \\sum_{a,b,c=0}^{7} (\\omega^a + \\omega^b + \\omega^c)^{512}\\]\" width=\"253\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Because 512 is divisible by 8, any term where \\( |\\omega^a + \\omega^b + \\omega^c| &lt; 2 \\) doesn't contribute to the overall terms \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\). The only triplets that count are magnitude 2.\n</p><p>Carrying this out gives \\( 3^3 \\) different full unital root contributions, and these are applied in \\( 2^{22} \\) different roots, for a total \\( 2^{22} \\times 27 \\) ways. \n</p><p>Our probability is then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/b/03b4cb7d60572c5595c0ef8306cab0013471496f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2^{21} \\times 7}{2^{22} \\times 27} = \\frac{7}{2 \\times 27} = \\boxed{\\frac{7}{54}}\\]\" width=\"201\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>Once again, this is an incredibly difficult solution. Before even attempting this, be sure you have a solid background in intermediate to Olympiad level algebra, precalculus, and counting/probability.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>WLOG, assume that the first two moves are equal for all possible combinations, since the direction does not matter. The first move has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601647ba43388e569f7f0f70d7d59201484f2e38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability of being along one of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> unit cubes around the origin, and the second move has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c78a56c7634e0570dc3f4bb300a874c15c0bc97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance.\nNow, there are two cases. We are currently on one of the points of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> squares that are aligned with the axes.\nThe first case is if the bee moves to the corner of a cube farthest away from the origin.\nHere, there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/4/894badd564b15babe2cbe70cfcabf7384c17b7c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> chance of this happening and a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/4/894badd564b15babe2cbe70cfcabf7384c17b7c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> chance of the fourth move remaining on one of the cubes.\nThe second case is if the bee moves along the same plane of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> squares previously, ending up on a point 1 away from the origin. There is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43ba19be0e30b99142416ebfbe17670437c8453e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance of this happening and a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/f/d8f1f9b224572d5d1cede49bdc8bdc95663cb309.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> chance of remaining on one of the cubes.\nNow, multiply and sum for the answer.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/1/231b6abb32e723df5468e5bd994af575c55d6dae.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot(\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{12})\\]\" width=\"259\" height=\"37\" />\nEvaluating this gives you the answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/9/43932adeab5cd9e028041cd6bf2062537827a95f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) }\\frac{7}{54}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\nSolution by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Juwushu\" title=\"User:Juwushu\">juwushu</a>.\nMinor edits by andliu766\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Remove the dice. It is not necessary for this problem. It is the same problem if you instead view this problem as the bee moving one up or down in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/5/0b56d32444d8efd65eb0cb6c01f8ac72c32587e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> directions.\n</p><p>Count the total number of ways for one cube of the 8 total ones. It is not hard to see that after the first step (which you can travel to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/2/78237119bf50a2698d27027ebf3d2d0f4f394ec0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0,0), (0,1,0),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/5/96521b1a203c3bb20a336e59cb63b1f0d232fb63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> -  3 ways), you have a total of 8 moves to traverse a face of that 1x1 cube. Hence, for each cube, you would have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6addcd59406afdfa8fdfc5fed8db2b4ec779010.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\cdot 3 = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"76\" height=\"14\" /> total moves.\n</p><p>There are a total of 8 cubes you could have traversed, though, so multiplying this by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/c/edc34826c6b0d3ee34b952b2ff088792935468ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 3.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>We have to account for overcounting. For example, when we traversed the face in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/d/2dd70347c7940f63ff9417e383548564fd92aff7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/d/31d6ff0912075ccd6db04899b044abbbea609bcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0), (0,0,1),(1,0,0),(1,0,1),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"261\" height=\"20\" /> the number of ways that we traverse that particular face got counted twice, once from the cube in the far upper right and once from the cube in the closer upper right.\n</p><p>For a given face, you can go clockwise or counterclockwise, hence yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways. There are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> shared faces, each one shared by exactly two faces so no additonal PIE is needed. We simply subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/5/c85650e45f5a1f03c1a201afceabc04e4e45638c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"42\" height=\"14\" /> from our original count now, yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/5/875457348f4bcef7a18a518e0b497afa3bb2659e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 8 - 2 \\cdot 12 = 168.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"175\" height=\"15\" /> \n</p><p>With no restrictions, there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/a/e8a50572356a684318fe212cf54d83e407626cdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" /> ways to move four steps. Your probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/a/28a76be983dd68d2f4db3c9ddacc23155fad3d2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{168}{6^4}=\\frac{7}{54}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"85\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An invariant is that the sum of all three coordinates change parity with every roll of the dice. Hence, to end up on a vertex of a unit cube, it can only be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/992be26a8d2b207f24cef4a13386cda421e070d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb469a4813ba1cc1aae00e5d449f3bfa0c344f34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37e4b3b309891ebb408b38b99d65c87d869d4f1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, and all the negative permutations of each listed (in total, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> different final coordinates can be reached).\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, we choose two of the three coordinates to have 'cancelling' operations. Let these be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1e51ef5511c6d27c703b84edc8ff93ffbc3717.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&#39;$\" width=\"13\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/9/d39574adf537bc19e989f7b69bee4bf19c7d7f1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b&#39;$\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> cancels out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1e51ef5511c6d27c703b84edc8ff93ffbc3717.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&#39;$\" width=\"13\" height=\"14\" />, hence cannot be placed next to each other, which would be traversing the same edge). There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/2/9f224755cdec91613cb7e6cabb03f85b31d09574.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\cdot2\\cdot2\\cdot2=24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" /> ways for this (we can only have forms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/6/b46df43b905c5d97224dca1aa29456fa5008cf4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$aba&#39;b&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/8/ac8ed9120b0240320be6092096c51d5a84cbd8ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$bab&#39;a&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> and their permutations among <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />).\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/992be26a8d2b207f24cef4a13386cda421e070d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, we must have two operations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> and a pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/1/f51ed22c1a97267ab79560425f4e72e3bcd64dda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c&#39;$\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Experimenting with having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of the same type of operation leads to finding a repeated edge no matter the arrangement. These can be arranged in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> ways (place the operations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> first, placing the operations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/1/f51ed22c1a97267ab79560425f4e72e3bcd64dda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c&#39;$\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the remaining spaces).\n</p><p>Analogously, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/1/8615f90fa1b89c0ef5c841647fe95adca6cb9839.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/2/2f21b12266189ec6518bb8e1f8a492219707aebb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,-1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/604101731eff1d7fa7f58724422a8da34d320e1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1,-1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"88\" height=\"20\" /> all have the same number of ways, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> each (they are merely a rotation of all possibilities of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/2/992be26a8d2b207f24cef4a13386cda421e070d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />). Furthermore, we can generalise this to all the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> negative permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb469a4813ba1cc1aae00e5d449f3bfa0c344f34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37e4b3b309891ebb408b38b99d65c87d869d4f1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, for a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/9/f291eb970f7897b73732c0a157f8fab7d06a5205.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12\\cdot4\\cdot3=144$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"116\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/7/e27858bd5fc68806d828a62b3875d341ce8d726e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{24+144}{6^4}=\\frac{7\\cdot24}{54\\cdot24}=\\frac{7}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"198\" height=\"38\" />, hence we pick <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/9/43932adeab5cd9e028041cd6bf2062537827a95f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) }\\frac{7}{54}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>-lisztepos\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2uTWPiWzfB0\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2uTWPiWzfB0</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy_.28Fast_and_Easy_.E2.9A.A1.EF.B8.8F.F0.9F.9A.80.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Pi_Academy_(Fast_and_Easy_⚡️🚀)\">Video Solution by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡️🚀)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡️🚀)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/3Dk-vmdxbrw?si=G6TcyLN2Rfwd2YYJ\">https://youtu.be/3Dk-vmdxbrw?si=G6TcyLN2Rfwd2YYJ</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Innovative_Minds\">Video Solution by Innovative Minds</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Innovative Minds\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/CxLGUPhYYDI\">https://youtu.be/CxLGUPhYYDI</a>\n</p><p>~i_am_suk_at_math_2\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ryqz2nVuG34?si=T3IU_aF8TJD1qmhT\">https://youtu.be/ryqz2nVuG34?si=T3IU_aF8TJD1qmhT</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4b_YLnyegtw&amp;t=5535s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4b_YLnyegtw&amp;t=5535s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagrams_2\">